{"hash":"238ec44f0796ecc7","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":80.276,"said":"You have come all the way down. Here, below the earth, you stand before Shri Mahakaleshwar, the Lord of Time. This is the Lord who rose from the earth for four faithful brothers, who heard a little boy’s grief, and who outlasted the ruin and the long wait. Every moment that has passed, and every one to come, belongs to him. Fold your hands, and let your thoughts grow still. The third verse of the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings: ‘Avantikayam vihitavataram muktipradanaya cha sajjananam, akalamrityoh parirakshanartham vande Mahakalamahasuresham.’ ‘He who took form in Avantika to grant liberation to the good, and to protect them from untimely death: I worship Mahakala, the great lord of the gods.’ Mahakal is the third Jyotirlinga of the Stotram, and today you stand closer to him than almost any pilgrim can. Now the drums and the damaru begin, the conch sounds, and the lamps rise before the south-facing lord. Let the aarti begin. Jai Shri Mahakal.\n\nDid you know? On the general darshan route you see Mahakal from the Ganesh Mandapam; entry to the sanctum itself is regulated by the temple committee.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1009},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1025,"end":1161}],"words":[[0.098,0.156,0,3],[0.266,0.192,4,8],[0.471,0.288,9,13],[0.771,0.264,14,17],[1.048,0.096,18,21],[1.156,0.18,22,25],[1.348,0.445,26,30],[2.692,0.397,32,36],[3.329,0.312,38,43],[3.654,0.156,44,47],[3.822,0.3,48,53],[4.363,0.168,55,58],[4.543,0.409,59,64],[4.964,0.349,65,71],[5.324,0.252,72,76],[5.589,0.962,77,90],[6.779,0.096,92,95],[6.887,0.3,96,100],[7.199,0.132,101,103],[7.344,0.457,104,108],[8.702,0.288,110,114],[9.002,0.108,115,117],[9.123,0.084,118,121],[9.219,0.337,122,126],[9.567,0.144,127,130],[9.724,0.3,131,135],[10.036,0.168,136,140],[10.216,0.192,141,144],[10.421,0.192,145,150],[10.625,0.168,151,154],[10.805,0.3,155